            <div xml:id="d30">

                <pb facs="0062" n="36" sameAs="frus:frus1872p2v5/pg36"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">No. 24.</note>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Confidential.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <head>Mr. Everett to Mr.
                        Calhoun.</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1845-04-02"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >April</hi> 2, 1845.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: * * * * * * *</p>
                <p>A person very high in the confidence of the government, but not belonging to it,
                    informed me a day or two since that he con*sidered the view of the Oregon
                    question lately delivered on the subject in Boston by Mr. William Sturgis as
                    fair and candid. <seg type="margin">Mr. Sturgis’s pamphlet regarded by a friend
                        of the British ministry as fair and candid.</seg><seg type="margin"
                        >[28]</seg></p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>EDWARD EVERETT.</signed>
                </closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">John C</hi>. <hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Calhoun</hi>, Esq., <hi rendition="#italic">Secretary of
                        State.</hi></p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
